decisions:
  - id: d-extract-university-core
    decision: Extract @a-company/university-core as a lean package (js-yaml+zod, zero @a-company deps); all 3 consumers import it via shims
    rationale: Fixed the same pack-loader bug 3x across 4 releases; the multi-tenant feature is evolving fast so duplication compounds. Lean (slim reimplemented discovery, not pack-loader) keeps the dep-light university package from gaining an internal-coupling chain.
  - id: d-safeloadpackid-home
    decision: safeLoadPackId lives in university-core, not CLI
    rationale: Spec §3's claim that loadOrFabricatePackManifest subsumes it was false (it fabricates pack_id from dir basename for partial manifests); core is its single canonical home per §5.5, avoiding re-introducing a deleted copy.
  - id: d-bundle-not-publish-only
    decision: university-core is published (private:false, in dependencies '*') AND bundled into consumers
    rationale: Matches the established premise-core pattern; consumers list it as a dep so npm install resolves it, even though tsup noExternal inlines the code. Must be published FIRST.
learnings:
  - 'The durable cure for ''same bug fixed N times'' is extraction into a shared package the consumers can all import — but package boundaries dictate WHERE it lives: the CLI can''t import paradigm-mcp, so the shared code had to become a new lean leaf package (zero @a-company deps) that everyone depends on, mirroring the premise-core/anchor-path precedent.'
  - 'A bundled-but-published package is the project''s pattern: *-core packages are private:false and listed in consumer dependencies with ''*'' (so npm install resolves them) AND bundled via tsup noExternal (so they work at runtime). A NEW such package must be published BEFORE its consumers or their install breaks.'
  - The highest-risk failure mode for bundled dynamic-import code (serve loads @a-company/university/server via import()) is the bundler leaving a workspace dep EXTERNAL — unit tests pass, runtime breaks. Gate it with a built-ARTIFACT smoke (launch serve from the linked CLI + curl /api/pack-config), not just unit green, plus a dist grep proving no bare specifier survives.
  - '''Behavior-neutral'' refactors still need a precise guarantee: split into read-path byte-identical (proven by moving code verbatim + diffing) vs enumerated intentional convergence deltas (the divergences the consolidation CLOSES). Burying a delta (e.g. CLI search now matches tags) violates the neutrality claim — enumerate every one.'
